      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:

** The current 6" chimney for the wood stove. By adding a dedicated combustion air inlet for the wood stove, you can eliminate the need for 6" chimney pipe with regards to the air leakage.
**  Fair bit of air is leaking through the frames of the older windows. I also suspect there is pink fibreglass insulation behind these frames. While pink fibreglass insulation is a good insulator, it is not effective at sealing air leaks. I recommend adding spray foam insulation beneath the frames whenever you are replace the older windows.This will enhance both insulation and air sealing capablities.
** The exterior doors - these doors are not sealing properly (can seal with weatherstripping).
** Majority of air leakage is occuring on the second floor of your home. The windows and the front door are inadequately sealed, allowing warm air to escape. I recommend applying caulking to seal any gaps or cracks around the frames of your windows, and also installing weatherstripping materials around the edges of windows and door to create a tight seal. These measures will help prevent air from leaking out.

Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 15% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 650 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 11.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <windows>
All the windows in this house (14 total) are a mixture of older single and double pane windows, that are in need of replacement. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR® certified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 700 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 12 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the Searchable product list of eligible windows for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.

ENERGY STAR labels must be affixed to windows at the time of installation and remain in place until the time of your post-retrofit evaluation. If ENERGY STAR labels are removed prior to the post-retrofit evaluation, the following photos are your responsibility to acquire, retain and provide to the energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation:
A photo of each newly-installed window showing a portion of the surrounding wall assembly from the inside, outside or both, AND

A photo of each ENERGY STAR label affixed to the window that clearly shows the ENERGY STAR certification mark, Manufacturer model code/number, U-factor/Energy Rating, NRCan reference number or a Certified Products Directory (CPD) number, and a Certified mark from one of the following:
? Canadian Standards Association (CSA)
? Intertek Canada
? Keystone Certifications
? Labtest Certification (LC)
? QAI Laboratories
? National Fenestration Council (NFRC)

Removed labels must be submitted and provided to the energy advisor.

Request your supplier/installer to include the following information on the invoice:
? Name of the homeowner and address of the house;
? Date of installation;
? Window make and model numbers;
? NRCan Reference Number;
? Indication of whether windows are ENERGY STAR or ENERGY STAR Most Efficient certified;
? Number of windows and location of each installation;
? U-factor/Energy Rating (ER);
? Itemized cost of each window and total cost of installation; and
? Itemization of any other work done as part of the installation, such as air/gap sealing and associated costs.

Please note that a window schedule (quote) cannot be used as a substitute for the aforementioned acceptable window validations and cannot be submitted to the portal as a standalone document to validate window installation and eligibility. However, the window schedule may be attached to the invoice provided there is a reference on the invoice to a schedule reference number to link the two documents.</windows>
                <doors>
Your second floor exterior door in this house is older hollow wood door. I recommend you upgrade this older door to an ENERGY STAR® certified door.

Rebates: By replacing this older door with one that is ENERGY STAR® certified, you could potentially save $ 130 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save energy and improve comfort. The door grant applies to the entire door system installed in a rough opening. A door system installed in a rough opening may consist of one or more doors and may include side lights and transoms. Side lights and transoms installed in the rough opening are not eligible for individual grants. To determine the eligibility of the product(s) you are seeking to purchase, confirm that each product is included on the Searchable product list of eligible doors for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.

ENERGY STAR labels must be affixed to each component of the door system at the time of installation and remain in place until the time of your post-retrofit evaluation. If ENERGY STAR labels are removed prior to the post-retrofit evaluation, the following photos are your responsibility to acquire, retain and provide to the energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation.

A photo of each newly-installed door system showing a portion of the surrounding wall assembly from the inside, outside or both, AND

A photo of each ENERGY STAR label affixed to each component of the door system that clearly shows the ENERGY STAR certification mark, Manufacturer model code/number, U-factor/Energy Rating, NRCan reference number or a Certified Products Directory (CPD) number, and a Certified mark from one of the following:
? Canadian Standards Association (CSA)
? Intertek Canada
? Keystone Certifications
? Labtest Certification (LC)
? QAI Laboratories
? National Fenestration Council (NFRC)

Removed labels must be submitted and provided to the energy advisor.

Request your supplier/installer to include the following information on the invoice:
? Name of the homeowner and address of the house;
? Date of installation;
? Make and model numbers of each door component (door leaf, side light, transom);
? NRCan Reference Number;
? Indication of whether door system components are ENERGY STAR certified;
? Number of door system components and location of each installation;
? U-factor/Energy Rating (ER);
? Itemized cost of each door system component and total cost of installation; and
? Itemization of any other work done as part of the installation, such as air/gap sealing and associated costs.

Please note that a door schedule (quote) cannot be used as a substitute for the aforementioned acceptable door systems validations and cannot be submitted to the portal as a standalone document to validate door systems installation and eligibility. However, as for windows, the door schedule may be attached to the invoice provided there is a reference on the invoice to a schedule reference number to link the two documents.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>
You currently have a 182 liter electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. As electricity is generated from diesel generators in your community, the overall efficiency is very low - diesel is used to produce electricity that is then used to produce your hot water. 

Savings: By replacing your electric hot water heater with an oil-fired instantaneous (on-demand) water heater with a energy factor of 0.71, you could potentially save $ 700 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with an oil-fired Toyotomi direct vent space heater with an efficiency of 87%. It seems that the space heater falls short in meeting the heating requirements of your entire house, prompting you to rely on a wood stove to heat the majority of your home. 

I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat and installing a dedicated combustion air inlet for the wood stove. To ensure the enitre house receives heat, you will need to add ductwork.I recommend getting in touch with a heating contractor who specializes in high efficiency, direct vent appliances. They will be able to provide the expertise and guidance needed.

Savings: By replacing your current oil-fired space heater with a 95% AFUE condensing oil furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower and installing a dedicated combustion air inlet for the wood stove, you could potentially save $ 520 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $3,500 is available for replacing an oil-fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified oil-fired furnace. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Wood Stove: You are currently using your woodstove for the majority of the heating season to heat your house, together with the Toyotomi Laser 73 as both are needed to satisfy the heating requirements of your home. As noted above, we recommend that you should have a dedicated outside combustion air pipe installed directly into the back of this woodstove. I am providing you with the following calculations to help you place a value on using the woodstove to offset oil use:

1. By reducing your use of the 81% efficient woodstove from 100% to 50% of the heating requirements of your house (and using a new 95% AFUE oil-fired furnace with the ECM blower motor), your annual energy costs are expected to increase by $2,600 per year in energy costs, compared to the $520 in annual net savings previously outlined above. 

2. By reducing your use of the 81% efficient woodstove from 100% to 0% of the heating requirements of your house (and using a new 95% AFUE oil-fired furnace with the ECM blower motor), your annual energy costs are expected to increase by $7,100 per year in energy costs, compared to the $520 in annual net savings previously outlined above.

The calculations above help you place a value on your efforts to harvest &amp; burn wood for the heating of your home. But, having a properly sized furnace that can meet the total heating requirements of your house would provide peace of mind knowing that if you needed to leave your house for a few weeks during the heating season, the new furnace could carry the full load and keep your house from freezing up.   
</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.

Savings: By installing a 4 kW solar photovoltaic system on the right facing roof of your house (orientation = SW), you could potentially save $ 1,100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount is based on the lower of:
1. 50% of the eligible cost of the system, up to a maximum of $20,000.
2. A calculation of $4.00 per watt that the system can produce.

Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Photovoltaic Solar Panels: Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,300 per kW DC is available for installing a solar photovoltaic (PV) system. If you choose to install a PV system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar PV installer, including the measurement of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. 

The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW DC. The system must be composed of PV panels and an inverter certified to CSA Standards. Solar panels can be mounted on the house or ground, as long as they are on the property / land of the house associated with the application. Building permits may be required for both off-grid and grid-connected installations. For more information, refer to Natural Resources Canadas Solar Ready Guidelines document that can be found online.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:
- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.
Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights  Non-hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Fort Good Hope, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.4 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace, domestic hot water tank and wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. 

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 
               - a clothes dryer

I did not see a rangehood fan and a bathroom exhaust fan in your house. Rangehood exhaust fan effectively remove odors and improve overall air qualty in your home. Bathroom exhaust fan efficiently removes excess humidity and moisture from bathing or showering, preventing the growth of mold and mildew. You may want to consider installing a bathroom exhaust fan with ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs  Fort Good Hope

The following Dec. 1, 2022 values, excluding GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Good Hope:

Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to subsidy limit, after subsidy $0.69 per kWh
Oil - $2.11 per liter 
Wood - $578 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

** Oil &amp; Biomass fuels in Fort Good Hope tend to experience cyclical fuel price volatility. The Arctic Energy Alliance therefore uses 5-year rolling average for these values to better help you evaluate longer term heating appliance purchase/upgrade decisions.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Abdul Mohammed, Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333</AdditionalComments>
